  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "mxD/1T/lQdBXHcHmu7FiM+CYsYM=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:28:58.649Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "Pacifico ships its own open-source framework, racletteJS, states an 'Open Source First' position, and holds OSBA and digital-sovereignty memberships. The research describes the ideological match as exact: the firm has already made the commitments the thesis assumes, so none of them has to be argued for.\n\nThe counterweight is size \u2014 a four-person shop. Both facts sit in the same sentence of the research deliberately, because ideological alignment and delivery capacity are separate axes and this lead scores at opposite ends of them. Neither fact cancels the other out; they decide different things.\n\nIn practice alignment decides whether a conversation is worth starting, and capacity decides what may be asked of it. A four-person shop that owns its framework can co-build a reference quickly, precisely because it controls its own code and has no committee to convince, and it cannot absorb volume. Recording both prevents the lead from being over-ranked on enthusiasm or dismissed on headcount.\n\n---\n\n## Leads sharing this finding (1)\n\n**2 \u00b7 next**\n- Pacifico Digital Explorations GmbH (DE Ottendorf-Okrilla (Dresden)) \u2014 Christian Silbermann + Michelle Fuchs, Gesch\u00e4ftsf\u00fchrer \u00b7 info@pacifico.cloud \u00b7 https://pacifico.cloud\n\nVerbatim research text this claim was extracted from:\n\n> Ships its own OSS framework (racletteJS), 'Open Source First', OSBA and digital-sovereignty member \u2014 the ideology matches exactly, but it is a 4-person shop.",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-19T11:28:58.649712Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:28:58.649716Z"
    }
  },
